{
  "dom_id": "questionContainer",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14097559/assets/07fd13a9e6e7c1c98e67385a6b7f0e2822962189076f5173dd1d4aa343335745.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above.",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14097559/assets/df5848bfb08f41036e8ef295a61023eefda252d0f0c32cd7fc64ec1f0b0d5c0b.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes become vertical as $y\\to -3$ only. \n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032} & =\\frac{1}{y+3} \\\\ {y}^{\u2032} & =-\\frac{1}{(y+3)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the line $y=-3$ divides the plane into $2$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $2$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<-3$ | $y>-3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $\u2198$ | $\u2197$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{1}{y+3}.\n$$",
